we've been recently seeing some new  rumors about apple's upcoming iphone 13  coming with an always on display and  surprisingly  i'm actually seeing quite a few doubters  on online forums like mac rumors  so what i'm gonna do in this video is  explain why the iphone 13 pro  will for sure be getting and always on  display  so let's get started first off if we're  talking about the iphone getting an  always on display  we have to discuss the product that  apple has already given that feature  too and that's of course the apple watch  with the apple watch series 5  apple introduced the always on display  which was a feature that many people  asked for  for many many years and now the big  question is  why did it take apple that long to give  the apple watch that feature  well apple themselves gave the credit to  the low temperature  polysilicon and oxide display which  features a reinvented  pixel architecture that lets the screen  refresh rate  dip from 60 hertz to a power sipping  one hertz when the watch is inactive and  just in case you don't understand how  screen refresh rate works  let me quickly explain standard displays  like the ones on the current iphone and  macbooks  use display technology with 60hz  refresh rate and that basically means  that for every second  the image on the display updates 60  times  and that's essentially how video works  as well it's just a bunch of images or  frames  updating or changing multiple times a  second for example  most movies and videos out there  including almost all of our videos  are recorded at 24 frames per second  which correlates with 24 hertz so the  image changes  24 times every single second which gives  it a more cinematic or  film-like look however a 24hz display  would look incredibly choppy for ui  elements  so that's why apple uses the standard  60hz displays  now there are also higher refresh rate  displays like the 144 or even  240hz gaming displays but they require  a lot more power to run which isn't a  problem for  a desktop pc but it does become a  problem for gaming laptops  draining battery life much more quickly  and all of that also applies to the  apple watch  which is why apple needed to get that  ltpo display  which can go down from 60 hertz to just  one  when in the standby always on mode  saving a massive amount of battery life  which is essential for the apple watch  now the truth is that the apple watch  series 4  actually already had an lcpo display so  they technically could have given it and  always on display  if they had the other features ready  like the low power driver and the  ambient light  sensor but in my opinion apple held off  on that feature  until the series 5 because if they  didn't  then there literally would be no reason  for anyone to upgrade  to the series 5 at all since there were  barely any other changes  but basically the main point i want you  to realize  is that an ltpo display is required  to give an oled smartphone variable  refresh  rate so now let's get into the iphone  last year we actually had rumors of the  iphone 12 pro getting an ltpo panel  with 120 hertz refresh rate but reports  later came out  claiming that because of battery life  concerns apple had to choose between  either 5g  or 120 hertz and they went with 5g  since they thought it would lead to more  sales and it turns out that they were  right  because they sold 100 million iphone 12s  in just seven months  but this year we're basically now 100  sure  that the iphone 13 pro will be getting a  120 hertz display  with many different leaks and rumors  from various sources  like the supply chain and reliable  leakers  going all the way back to july of 2020  display analyst ross young let us know  that ltvo display  would not be coming to the iphone 12 pro  and he was right  then in december last year the elect  claimed that two out of four iphone 13  models  will come with ltbo displays needed to  enable 120hz refresh rate  pointing to both the pro models  exclusively  then a couple of months later max  weinbach claimed that the iphone 13  would be getting a 120 hertz  promotion display and this is actually  the first leak we have  that pointed to an always on display  feature coming  but i'll get into that in just a minute  in march ming cha quo also agreed  that the iphone 13 pro models will be  getting  120hz displays then in april digitimes  made an interesting claim  that the iphone 13 pro models will  feature 120 hertz promotion refresh rate  while at the same time using 15 to 20  percent  less power consumption and if you're  finding that hard to believe  i'll explain how it's gonna work in just  a second but going further into their  report  they said that both samsung display and  lg display  are converting their supply lines to  offer  ltpo displays at their dedicated apple  manufacturing lines  with the conversion likely complete in  the first half of 2021  and apparently just one month later the  elect reported that samsung  began production of 120 hertz ltpo  displays  for the iphone 13 pro models while lg  began production of  regular ltps displays for the lower end  iphone 13s and then just yesterday  mark german who was the most reliable  apple leaker out there  finally chimed in on this topic  mentioning that the iphone 13 will  potentially get  120 hertz and in always on mode  basically confirming that the 13 pro  will for sure  get 120 hertz no doubt about it but the  weird thing is that he mentioned that  the display will give you  better battery life so let me explain  how that's going to be possible  while also being able to go up to 120  hertz in 2017  apple revealed the 10.5 inch ipad pro  with 120 hertz pro  motion technology and the way it works  is by automatically  adjusting the refresh rate between 24  hertz and  120 hertz depending on the content that  you're viewing  so if you're on the homepage you're  getting super smooth  120 hertz but if you're watching a  youtube video filmed at 24 fps  like this one is the refresh rate goes  down to 24 hertz  and that actually saves a ton of battery  life  compared to other ipads that are always  stuck  at 60 hertz so basically the new iphone  13 pro  will come with this same tech so  watching one of our 24 fps videos  will help you save battery life which is  great  but it still won't be good enough to  enable always  on mode because of that i personally  believe that the display refresh rate  will actually be able to go down to just  one hertz  just like the apple watch and that is  what's gonna enable  and always on mode on the iphone 13 pro  models  and the reason i believe that is because  samsung's latest galaxy s21 ultra  and the new oneplus 9 pro both have  displays that can adjust between  1 and 120 hertz and oneplus  actually mentioned the scenarios when  it'll hit one hertz  and they said that whenever you look at  a static image  it goes down to one saving tons of  battery life  so basically you can expect the iphone  13 pros always on display  to work just like it does on the apple  watch with the display  dimming down and turning some of the  display elements black  while having an outline clear enough to  read things like the time  and other important info and i know that  some people were worried about display  burn-in but that is not going to be a  problem because apple has software that  can dim down individual pixels  to avoid burning and of course the  display will already be so  dim that burn-in isn't likely to happen  in the first place  so to sum up this entire video the ltpo  display technology  is the secret sauce that enables  variable refresh rate  so if apple is going to give the iphone  13 pro models  120 hertz promotion then they can very  easily give it an  always-on mode at the same time as long  as it can go down to one hertz  like the modern android smartphones  already can and i personally  think it will because of all the leaks  and rumors we've seen from max weinbach  and the very reliable mark german and if  by any chance this feature  does not come this year it's because  apple decided to hold off  just to make next year's iphone 14 pro  seem a bit better but i personally think  that we're getting it this year now if  you totally disagree with me  then go ahead and let me know down in  the comment section below but if you  enjoyed this video  definitely click the circle above to  subscribe and check out one of those two  videos right over there  thanks for watching and we'll see you in  the next video

